// The zstd package: Zstandard (RFC 8878) decompression and compression (at
// levels 1 to 3), with its xxHash64, streaming in bounded pieces both ways
// and in one call. Written in Cone, with no C library and no call of the
// OS's.
//
//   mod squeeze;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import zstd;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     let packed = zstd.compress("the quick brown fox", 3);   // one zstd frame
//     match zstd.decompress(packed.view(), 0usize) {         // every frame, joined
//       case let Ok(text) { printInt(i64.from(text.len())); }  // 19
//       case let Err(e) { printStr(e.reason.view()); return 1i32; }
//     }
//     0i32
//   }
//
// It reads as package deflate does: a value that holds a stream's whole
// state, fed and pulled in bounded pieces, and one-call helpers beside it.
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   ONE CALL         the data in memory, the answer out
//     compress(data, level) List[u8]       one frame, with the content size and checksum
//     decompress(data, expected) Result[List[u8], ZstdError]
//     decompressWithDictionary(data, dictionary, expected) Result[List[u8], ZstdError]
//   'level' is 1, 2 or 3 (below 1 acts as 1, above 3 as 3).
//   'expected' is the output size if known (0 if not): it reserves room and is
//   also the most output allowed (0 allows 1 GiB), so a stream that claims to be
//   small and is not is refused as TooLarge. Every frame in 'data' is decoded
//   and their contents joined, skippable frames skipped, as 'zstd -d' does; no
//   input at all is no frames, an empty output.
//
//   STREAMING        a value that holds the whole state; any chunking
//     Decompressor.make()
//       feed(&Array[u8])          input, any size, split anywhere (copied)
//       finish()                  no more input will come
//       pull(&mut List[u8], max) Status    appends at most 'max' bytes of output
//       pull(&mut IoBuf) Status   appends into the buffer's spare room, at most
//                                 its room (the Transform of package stream)
//       setLimit(bytes), setMaxWindow(bytes), setDictionary(&content),
//       setMultiFrame(bool), error(), frameCount(), totalIn(), totalOut(),
//       isDone(), takeRest()
//     Compressor.make(level)
//       feed, finish, pull as above; totalIn(), totalOut(), isDone()
//     Status (package stream's, one for every transform): NeedInput (use up
//     what you have: feed more or finish), MoreOutput (pull again), Done, Failed
//     (a Decompressor only; 'error()' says why).
//     Both meet 'stream.Transform' (feed, pull into an IoBuf, finish), so a
//     stage runs either without knowing which.
//     'examples/tour.cone' feeds five bytes at a time and takes eight out, and
//     compresses a text in pieces.
//
//   CHECKSUM
//     xxh64(data, seed) u64, Xxh64 (make(seed), update(data, from, to),
//     digest()) to go on across pieces. A frame's content checksum is the low
//     32 bits of xxh64(content, 0).
//
//   TYPES
//     ZstdError {kind, offset, reason}   kind is an ErrorKind: BadHeader,
//         WindowTooLarge, NeedsDictionary, BadBlock, BadTable, BadSequence,
//         BadOffset, Truncated, BadChecksum, TooLarge; 'offset' is the input
//         bytes used before the unit that failed (a frame header, a block, a
//         checksum)
//
// WHAT IS DECODED: all of RFC 8878's frame format. Frames: the magic number,
// the frame header (window descriptor, dictionary ID, content size in 1, 2, 4
// or 8 bytes, single segment), the content checksum; skippable frames (all 16
// magic numbers); any number of frames in a row. Blocks: raw, RLE and
// compressed. Literals: raw and RLE (header of 1, 2 or 3 bytes), Huffman-coded
// in 1 or 4 streams (every size format), the tree by direct 4-bit weights or
// FSE-coded weights, and "treeless" (the frame's last tree again). Sequences:
// the 1-, 2- and 3-byte counts; for each of literal lengths, offsets and match
// lengths the predefined, RLE, FSE-coded and repeat modes; the three repeat
// offsets, as the RFC moves them (a literal length of 0 shifting them by one).
// The window, and a raw-content dictionary.
//
// WHAT IS WRITTEN: one frame a stream. Its header: the magic number, the
// descriptor (content checksum always on, no dictionary ID), single segment
// when the content size is known and no larger than the level's window (else
// the window descriptor), the content size when known, in 1, 2, 4 or 8
// bytes. Blocks of 128 KiB, the last shorter: RLE (one byte value; never the
// frame's first block), compressed, or raw, whichever is smallest. Literals:
// raw, RLE, or Huffman-coded in 1 stream (under 256 literals) or 4, the tree
// by 4-bit weights or FSE-coded weights, with headers of every size.
// Sequences: the 1-, 2- and 3-byte counts; for each of literal lengths,
// offsets and match lengths the predefined, RLE or FSE-coded table; the three
// repeat offsets used wherever one fits, as the RFC moves them. Then the
// checksum. Never written: skippable frames, dictionaries, "treeless"
// literals and the "repeat" table mode (each block's tables are its own).
//
// STREAMING, IN A SENTENCE EACH
//
//   A Decompressor decodes one unit at a time: a magic number, a frame
//   header, a block with its 3-byte header (at most 128 KiB), a checksum. A
//   unit is decoded only once all of its bytes are in, so input can be cut
//   anywhere, a byte at a time, and nothing is half-done. A block is decoded
//   whole, into the history, and handed out in pieces of at most 'max' bytes;
//   the next block is decoded only when that one has all been handed out. A
//   frame may be followed by another, so the stream is Done only after
//   'finish' (or, with 'setMultiFrame(false)', at the first frame's end). The
//   Decompressor's own memory is the input not yet used, the window (all of
//   the frame's output while it is no longer than the window), one block, and
//   as much again as the window before it lets the old output go.
//
//   A Compressor writes a block once 128 KiB and one byte more are in, or the
//   input is finished, so every block but the last is full and the last is
//   known to be last; a block's bytes depend only on the data, never on how it
//   was cut or pulled (a test cuts it three ways at every size and shape and
//   compares). The frame header goes out with the first block and carries the
//   content size if 'finish' came before that: always for 128 KiB or less, and
//   whenever the whole input was fed before the first pull (as 'compress'
//   does). The Compressor's own memory is the input not yet compressed, the
//   window behind it (and as much again before it lets the old input go), its
//   hash tables (64 KiB at level 1, 256 KiB at 2, 768 KiB at 3; smaller for a
//   small input whose size is known) and the output not yet pulled.
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says what
// was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - Decoding is RFC 8878's, as the reference decoder (zstd's
//   lib/decompress) does it, without its speed tricks: an FSE table of
//   (symbol, bits, base) per state; a Huffman table indexed by the next
//   maxBits bits that answers one symbol per look; a backward bit reader that
//   reads zeros past the start of its bytes and counts them. Every bitstream
//   must decode to exactly its end, as the reference requires: bits left over,
//   or more read than there are, fail the block. The Huffman streams are
//   decoded one after another (the reference decodes the four at once,
//   interleaved, two symbols a look); the sequences are executed as they are
//   decoded; every copy is a byte at a time.
// - Streaming by whole units, not by a state machine that can stop at any
//   bit. The cost: a block's bytes are kept until all of them have come (at
//   most 128 KiB), and decoded at once. The gain: one decoder for one-call and
//   streamed use, and a hot loop with no stops in it.
// - The window: the largest one a frame may ask for is 128 MiB at first
//   (setMaxWindow), as the zstd tool's default (ZSTD_WINDOWLOG_LIMIT_DEFAULT,
//   2^27); a frame asking for more is refused with WindowTooLarge before
//   anything of it is decoded. The one-call helpers do not apply it: they keep
//   the whole output anyway, so a window costs them nothing (the reference's
//   one-call ZSTD_decompress does not apply it either); their bound is the
//   output limit. A single-segment frame's window is its content size.
// - A match may reach back as far as the window (RFC 8878 section 3.1.1.1.2),
//   and no further: an offset past it is BadOffset. (The reference decoder is
//   more lenient: it takes any offset within the output it holds; the tests
//   show it accepting one 1 byte past the window.) With a dictionary, matches
//   may reach into it while the frame's output is no longer than the window,
//   as the reference compressor uses a dictionary (it stops when a block ends
//   past the window).
// - A block decodes to at most the block maximum (the window, at most 128
//   KiB), compressed or not, as the reference requires.
// - A Huffman tree's codes are at most 11 bits (RFC 8878 section 4.2.1; the
//   reference allows 12), and, as the reference requires, it has at least two
//   codes of the longest length.
// - A bomb stopper: output is capped at 1 GiB unless 'setLimit' (or the
//   one-call 'expected') says otherwise, as deflate's is; a frame header that
//   declares more is refused at once. The room reserved up front is never
//   more than 64 MiB on a header's word.
// - Dictionaries: raw content only (zstd's "rawContent" mode: the bytes are
//   taken as content that comes before each frame, the repeat offsets start at
//   1, 4 and 8). A frame that names a dictionary by ID is refused with
//   NeedsDictionary, with or without a dictionary given (a raw-content
//   dictionary has no ID to match, as the reference refuses it). zstd's own
//   dictionary format (magic EC30A437, entropy tables and repeat offsets) can
//   follow.
// - Empty input is no frames and decodes to nothing (as ZSTD_decompress); a
//   frame cut anywhere is Truncated; bytes after a frame that are not another
//   frame are BadHeader.
// - xxHash64 is here, not in cryptohash: it is not a cryptographic hash, zstd
//   is its only user, and this lane adds one package and touches no other.
//   It takes 32 bytes a step in four lanes (Yann Collet's XXH64).
// - The tables of RFC 8878 (literal and match length codes, the three
//   predefined distributions) are immutable module-level arrays (tables.cone),
//   as deflate's RFC 1951 tables are. Everything else is in the values that
//   use it: nothing is shared between two Decompressors, so one per thread or
//   actor is safe; one is not safe to share.
// - The block decoder takes the input as '&List[u8]', not as a slice: the
//   borrow checker (error 1339) refuses a slice of one field of the
//   Decompressor alongside '&mut' to the others, which change shape, as it
//   cannot tell the fields apart; a '&List[u8]' lends no elements across the
//   call. The compressor's matchers and literal writer read their input the
//   same way, for the same reason (a slice of the input held while a List of
//   sequences grows is refused), taking an 8-byte slice only inside a helper.
//
// Compressing:
//
// - The levels are the reference's: its matcher and its parameters at each
//   level, as libzstd 1.5.7's ZSTD_getCParams answers for an input of unknown
//   size (and any over 256 KiB). Level 1: the fast matcher, a 2^19 window, a
//   2^14 table of the 7 bytes at a position. Level 2: the fast matcher, 2^20,
//   a 2^16 table of 6 bytes. Level 3: the double-fast matcher, 2^21, a 2^17
//   table of 8 bytes and a 2^16 table of 5. The matchers are the shape of
//   zstd's zstd_fast.c and zstd_double_fast.c (matcher.cone says what they
//   do) without their unrolling: the reference looks at two positions a
//   step and prefetches. A hash-chain matcher (the reference's levels 5 and
//   up) was left for the higher levels.
// - Each block's literals and sequences are coded on their own, the cheapest
//   way by an estimate of the bits (cblock.cone): no table is carried from
//   the block before (the reference reuses the last Huffman tree and FSE
//   tables when cheaper), so a raw block costs nothing to the next one but
//   the repeat offsets, which are put back. The reference's gain from reuse
//   is the tables' descriptions, tens of bytes a block.
// - FSE counts are normalized by rounding each to the table and giving the
//   difference to (or taking it one at a time from) the most frequent; no
//   "less than 1" counts are written (the reference's FSE_normalizeCount
//   uses them and a finer rounding). The accuracy is the reference's
//   FSE_optimalTableLog. Huffman lengths are Huffman's merge of the two
//   lightest (two queues), limited to 11 bits by halving the counts until
//   they fit (the reference's HUF_setMaxHeight moves lengths instead).
// - A block is compressed only if that saves 1/64 of it and 2 bytes, literals
//   are Huffman-coded only if that saves 1/64 of them and 2 bytes, and fewer
//   than 64 literals are written raw: the reference's thresholds at these
//   levels. A block of one byte value is RLE, but not as the frame's first
//   (as the reference does, for the sake of some older decoders).
// - The content checksum is always written, as the zstd tool does (the
//   library's default is off); there is no setting to leave it out.
// - The hash tables hold positions in the input buffer and are made with the
//   first block, smaller for a small input of known size (the reference's
//   ZSTD_adjustCParams: a table's log at most one more than the size's). When
//   the buffer drops input the window no longer reaches, every position moves
//   down with it; one that falls off becomes 0, a candidate that is checked
//   like any other. A match is taken only when its bytes compare equal and it
//   is within the window, so no table entry can make a wrong one.
// - Levels below 1 act as 1, above 3 as 3 (the reference has negative levels
//   and up to 22: later).
//
// TESTS. Made by the reference, libzstd 1.5.7 as Git for Windows installs it
// (C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libzstd.dll, SHA-256
// b95c223a9548a9ecf51377c962e0bc8f0c51eb0c6f67a296dbc885996f0dd40d), called
// through Python's ctypes only to MAKE test data (tests/data/zstdref.py pins
// it; nothing runs it at test time): tests/data/make_corpus.py writes
// corpus.bin (levels 1, 3, 9, 19 and 22, long-distance mode, streamed frames,
// every block, literal and sequence mode, tiny and empty inputs, many frames,
// a dictionary), and make_handmade.py writes handmade.bin and malformed.bin
// with its own small encoder, each frame checked against the reference. The
// tests decode every frame in one call and streamed every way, refuse every
// malformed one with the right kind split at every offset, flip every bit of
// some frames, and check xxHash64 against the reference's own.
//
// The compressor's tests: tests/roundtrip.cone compresses five shapes of data
// at 19 sizes around a block's edges, at every level, in one call and fed
// in pieces of 1, 997 and 65536 bytes, and decodes every frame with this
// package's decoder, in one call and streamed; the streamed frames must hold
// the one-call frame's blocks; matches at the window's far edge are taken
// and one byte past it are not, as the input buffer slides. The reference
// checks the same writer: tests/data/crosscheck.py makes a seeded corpus
// (text, random bytes, runs, binary records, inputs made to force every
// block, literal and sequence mode, matches at each level's window edge, 5 MB
// of mixed data) and has examples/crosscheck.cone compress it at levels 1 to
// 3, in one call and streamed two ways; libzstd 1.5.7 decodes each of the 336
// frames to its input byte for byte, and so does this package's decoder. It
// also makes two of roundtrip.cone's inputs, whose frames' XXH64 that test
// pins. The script is run by hand when the compressor changes (congo test
// runs no Python).
//
// OTHER COPIES. The repository was searched (Cone sources in packages/,
// samples/, tools/, compiler/) for another zstd, FSE, Huffman-literal or
// xxHash decoder: there is none. For the encoder: no other FSE encoder; the
// one Huffman length builder is deflate's (private to it, the same merge
// done by repeated search, limited to 15 bits); http2's HPACK code is a fixed
// table. This package does not import deflate for it.
//
// NOT HERE: levels above 3 (lazy, hash-chain and optimal matchers) and the
// negative levels; long-distance matching; compressing with a dictionary;
// writing "treeless" literals and repeated tables; zstd's own dictionary
// format and dictionary training; the legacy formats before RFC 8878 (zstd
// 0.x); reading from a file or a socket (the caller feeds it); multithreaded
// decoding or compression.
//
// SPEED, best of 5 runs on a loaded 13th-gen Core i7-13700HX, release build,
// in MB/s of the decompressed bytes, next to the reference library
// (libzstd 1.5.7, Git for Windows' build, called once per file through
// Python's ctypes) on the same files, made by the reference at levels 3 and
// 19 (examples/bench.cone makes the first columns):
//
//                                  one call    streamed    reference
//   Cone's packages' sources L3      257         218          526
//     (17.7 MB)              L19     308         237          540
//   conec.exe, first 16 MB   L3      181         160          508
//                            L19     175         140          303
//   pbrxrt.spv (385 KB)      L3      212         154          385
//                            L19     114          87          279
//
// So it runs at 35 to 60 per cent of the reference's speed. The likely cause
// is the copying: every literal and every match byte is moved one at a time
// through a List (the reference copies 16 or 32 bytes a step, overlapping
// past the end), and a streamed pull moves each byte once more. Then the
// Huffman streams decoded one at a time, one symbol a look, and the bit reader
// refilled a byte at a time. These are measured, not chased: nothing here has
// been tuned for speed past the shape of the algorithms.
// XXH64 runs at 6.7 GB/s.
//
// COMPRESSING, on the same three files (the reference's level-3 frames
// above, decoded), measured under about 74 per cent machine load (other
// work running), each program pinned to the same core at high priority,
// ours and the reference's taken in turn file by file; median of 5 runs in
// MB/s of the input, and the size against the reference's at the same level
// (libzstd 1.5.7, ZSTD_compress2 and ZSTD_compressStream2 through Python's
// ctypes, the checksum on as ours; examples/compressbench.cone makes ours):
//
//                             one call     streamed     reference      size
//                                                     one call  str.   vs ref
//   Cone's packages'   L1       188          192        511    454   +2.9%
//     sources          L2       181          175        447    396   +3.6%
//     (17.7 MB)        L3       159          157        347    315   +1.5%
//   conec.exe,         L1       130          129        493    430   +0.1%
//     first 16 MB      L2       124          123        408    345   +0.4%
//                      L3        85*          76        269    234   +0.4%
//   pbrxrt.spv         L1       158          154        482    405   +4.8%
//     (385 KB)         L2       152          145        387    320   +4.2%
//                      L3       132          127        311    281   +0.2%
//   (* runs from 72 to 110: not steady. The other rows' runs are within 10
//   per cent of each other.)
//
// So it runs at 26 to 50 per cent of the reference's speed, and its frames
// are 0.1 to 4.8 per cent larger. The likely causes of the time: input read
// a byte at a time through the List (a 4- or 8-byte read is one slice and one
// check, but each position still costs several), every literal copied a byte
// at a time into the block's literals and again through the Huffman writer
// one symbol a call, and the reference's two-positions-a-step loop not done.
// Of the size: no table reused from the block before, a coarser FSE
// normalization, and the fast matcher's simpler stepping. These are measured,
// not chased.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   zstd.cone          this overview
//   types.cone         ErrorKind, ZstdError
//   tables.cone        the constants of RFC 8878
//   xxhash.cone        xxh64, Xxh64
//   fse.cone           the bit readers, FSE table descriptions and tables
//   huffman.cone       Huffman tree descriptions, tables and streams
//   block.cone         a compressed block: literals, sequences, their tables
//   decompressor.cone  Decompressor: frames, blocks, the window, streaming
//   decompress.cone    decompress, decompressWithDictionary
//   encode.cone        the bit writer, FSE tables and counts, Huffman codes
//   matcher.cone       the fast and double-fast matchers: a block's sequences
//   cblock.cone        a compressed block: literals, sequences, their tables
//   compressor.cone    Compressor: the frame, blocks, the window, streaming
//   compress.cone      compress

mod zstd;

import collections use *;
import iobuf use IoBuf;
pub import stream use Status;
